    "notes": [
      "MAR row CM-43 S3 (F-CURR-002 section 9.2, 2026-10-30; passes: author, language, culture and facts, learning design, reviser, third pass; the three checkers ran as separate lenses in the authoring session, and an independent review agent then checked the content and the es and ko strings in the PR review stage, as the separate checker pass; no native-speaker or expert review is claimed).",
      "Scope: Volume 2 of Food & Daily Life at Stage 3: taste (맛이 ___ with 달아요 and 매워요, beside Volume 1's 맛있어요), preference and request (김밥을 좋아해요 and the frame ___ 주세요) and the steps of bibimbap in order (___을/를 넣어요, 먼저, 그다음에 비벼요). One new pattern a quest (U-3). Culture: no UNESCO, designation or origin claim; no real person, restaurant, city, brand or price; no age wording.",
      "Language: 해요체 with sentence marks; 물 주세요 and 김밥 주세요 drop the particle, as a request does; 그다음 is one word (Standard Korean Language Dictionary 그-다음), so 그다음에; particle rounds test 이/가 and 을/를 (떡볶이가, 라면이, 김밥을, 나물을, 고추장을, 비빔밥을); romanization by the converter (masi darayo, joahaeyo, neoeoyo, geudaeume bibyeoyo).",
      "Language (cont.): Every word-gap Cloze keeps tiles that are grammatical but wrong for the meaning line (맛이 매워요 against It tastes sweet, 물 마셔요 against Water, please), read by its meaning, as in Volume 1.",
      "Learning design: hasSoundChange flags 넣어요 [너어요], 좋아해요 [조아해요] and 떡볶이 [떡뽀끼]; liaison is not flagged, so 맛이 [마시], 맛있어요 [마시써요], 달아요 [다라요], 김밥을 [김바블] and 달걀을 [달갸를] are kept out of in-quest Dictation by authoring only (review may still offer 달아요, as the repo liaison policy allows). Dictation asks 빵 and 떡 only; q2 heard rounds answer with pictures.",
      "Learning design (cont.): Sentence Builder as sequence: q3 Practice walks the recipe in order (rice first, then 고추장, then mix, then the taste, then I like bibimbap; 나물 is put in by Discover and Apply a2), and the order is tested, not only shown: Apply a4 gaps the step word (___ 고추장을 넣어요 against Next, with 그다음에 and 먼저 as tiles).",
      "Learning design (cont.): Triples q1 order-it / cloze-pick / type-hangul, q2 order-it / listen-pick / cloze-pick, q3 order-it / cloze-pick / order-it; none equals a Volume 1 triple (every Volume 1 Apply is Pic-Word Match) or its neighbour; every Practice is a Sentence Builder, so each can scaffold (CM-24).",
      "Learning design (cont.): Practice and Check recycle 57, 50 and 43 in 100 (lesson-recycle); new units 3 a quest. Findings applied: q2 and q3 first recycled 33 in 100, so q2 Practice r5 became 떡볶이가 매워요 and q3 Check c1 밥이 뜨거워요 (Volume 1); when the independent review moved q3 Check c2 to the new 달걀을 넣어요, q3 Practice r5 took q2 비빔밥을 좋아해요 in place of 나물을 넣어요.",
      "Culture and facts: n1 (나물), n2 (two ways to serve 비빔밥) and n3 (the first known written recipe, a Hangul manuscript of the late 1800s) each matched phrase by phrase to the National Folk Museum of Korea's Encyclopedia of Korean Folk Culture (한국의식주생활사전, 한류문화사전) and the Standard Korean Language Dictionary, named as of the access date.",
      "Reviser: q2 first had two frames (U-3 allows one): ___ 주세요 stays the frame and 좋아해요 is taught by two sentences with a caption; Dictation asks 빵, not 김치, whose meaning kimchi spells its sound; n1 says plants and leaves; the q3 r1 distractor is 와요, which cannot follow 밥을. Third pass: validate-content, lesson lint, U-rules and overlay rules clean.",
      "Translation (es, ko): es keeps the Volume 1 wording (marca de objeto, después de consonante final; pastel de arroz; fideos instantáneos) and the cell's own picture labels; ko glosses are short definitions that never echo the Korean they explain (overlay-circular-gloss); the {ko:} tokens of each note and Discover line match the English.",
